# Design Spec & Chaos Test: Atlas Core Mobile Navigation Overhaul

**Author:** Mint Ito (Design / Chaos Testing)
**Project:** Atlas Core
**Referenced Materials:** Business Document: Company Document

## 1. Executive Summary & Context
In accordance with the functional guidelines set in the `Business Document: Company Document`, the mobile navigation for Atlas Core has been refactored to streamline access across SaaS and face-to-face service modules. This document outlines the stress tests, edge cases, and design specifications implemented to prevent layout degradation under chaotic usage patterns.

## 2. Resource Utilization
- **Business Document: Company Document**: Used to align core user journey hierarchies, verify role-based navigation permissions, and ensure critical service discovery paths meet organizational SLA thresholds under constrained screen viewports.

## 3. Chaos Test Scenarios & Edge Case Handling
- **Rapid Multi-Touch Interception**: Implemented debounce logic on the mobile drawer toggle (300ms) to eliminate state race conditions during frantic tapping.
- **Extreme Viewport Constraints (Foldables & Low-Res)**: Verified fluid UI rendering between 280px (narrow) and 820px (unfolded) widths without horizontal overflow or clipping.
- **Network Jitter & Dynamic Menus**: Navigation state maintains cached skeletons when background synchronization with SaaS tenant configurations stalls.
- **Deep Hierarchy Traversal**: Handled >4 nested sub-menu levels via sliding accordion trays rather than fly-outs to prevent touch-target collision on touchscreens.

## 4. Implementation Validation
- Gesture conflict matrix resolved for swipe-to-close vs. horizontal carousel scrolls.
- Target tap sizes enforced at minimum 48x48dp across all dynamic items.
```